problems.diagnosis:
REPRODUCED 2026-08-23 by direct probe (scripts/toolloop/memory_false_absence_probe.py), and its remaining tool is blocked behind a SECOND defect that is not this problem's.

THE REPRODUCTION, on a throwaway project:
    memory_remember(fact_text='Emberfall <nonce> ...', fact_type='statement')
      -> {'remembered': true, 'fact_id': '00b592a841c7655ffe94cdcf23928a1c'}
    memory_search(query='Emberfall <nonce>')     [immediately, and again after 3s]
      -> {'hits': [], 'count': 0, 'total': 0, 'degraded': {'semantic': 'not_indexed'}}
    memory_forget(fact_id='00b592...')  -> {'invalidated': true}

The invariant word for word: the store ACCEPTED the write and confirmed it with an id, and the read that surfaces it returns nothing - for the exact nonce the write had just stored.

TWO THINGS THE PROBE SETTLES. (1) The read is HONEST about being broken - degraded + a note, not a bare empty list - which is strictly better than the catalogue instance of the same shape (44 public books, items: [], no marker, DQ-T38), and suggests the fix here may be in how an ANSWER is composed from a degraded read rather than in the store. (2) The failure is the SEARCH path, not forget: handed the id directly, forget works. So memory_forget's membership here is precise - what the false absence breaks is the only route a model has to the fact_id, since memory_remember's id is gone by the next turn.

WHY THE TOOL IS NOT MOVED OUT. Its ledger row blocks it on a repeat forget RE-STAMPING valid_until on an already-invalidated fact - idempotency, not false absence, and it would block the tool even if this problem were fixed tomorrow. But the false absence reproduced today is genuinely one of its blockers, and moving it would strand this problem's memory instance with no tool carrying it. Recorded as a tool with two defects rather than forced into one bucket.
